    "content": "<p>India is among the most important global sources of handmade rugs, and for good reason: skilled labour, a wide range of constructions and the flexibility to produce custom designs. But sourcing wholesale from overseas can be intimidating the first time. This guide walks you through the process step by step, from defining your needs to receiving your first shipment. It is written by Tajmahal Products, a Bhadohi-based manufacturer and exporter serving buyers in North America, Europe, Australia and the Middle East.</p>\n<h2>Step 1: Define what you want to buy</h2>\n<p>Before you contact any supplier, get clear about:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Construction:</strong> hand-knotted, hand-tufted, handwoven, or a mix.</li><li><strong>Style:</strong> traditional, Mughal, tribal, Moroccan, Gabbeh, contemporary.</li><li><strong>Material:</strong> wool, silk, blends, viscose.</li><li><strong>Sizes:</strong> standard sizes for your market, runners, round rugs or custom dimensions.</li><li><strong>Price positioning:</strong> entry, mid-range or premium.</li><li><strong>Volume and frequency:</strong> one-off order or an ongoing programme.</li></ul>\n<p>The clearer your brief, the easier it is for a manufacturer to quote accurately and to propose the right products.</p>\n<h2>Step 2: Find the right type of supplier</h2>\n<p>There are three broad types of suppliers: direct manufacturers, exporters who source from many workshops, and trading agents. Direct manufacturers generally offer the most control over quality and customisation. Exporters may offer wider ranges. Whichever you choose, ask who actually makes the product and where.</p>\n<h2>Step 3: Check credentials</h2>\n<p>Reasonable checks include:</p>\n<ul><li>Years in business and export history.</li><li>Government-recognised export status.</li><li>Membership of trade bodies such as the Carpet Export Promotion Council.</li><li>Social or ethical-trade certifications.</li><li>Customer references in your region.</li></ul>\n<p>Tajmahal Products, for example, has been in the international export market since 1967 and holds recognised export credentials.</p>\n<h2>Step 4: Request catalogues and samples</h2>\n<p>Review catalogues first, then request physical samples of the qualities you are interested in. Samples let you judge pile feel, colour, finish and construction. For custom designs, ask for a strike-off or a sample piece to approve before bulk production. Never skip this step: a signed-off sample is your reference if there is a dispute later.</p>\n<h2>Step 5: Write specifications</h2>\n<p>A specification should include design reference, size, construction, materials, knot count or density where relevant, pile height, colours and tolerance for size variation. Written specifications protect both buyer and seller.</p>\n<h2>Step 6: Understand MOQ and lead times</h2>\n<p>Minimum order quantities depend on construction and customisation. Hand-knotted carpets take longer to produce than hand-tufted rugs, so lead times differ. Ask for realistic timelines, and add buffer for shipping and any seasonal deadlines.</p>\n<h2>Step 7: Agree pricing and terms</h2>\n<p>Be clear on:</p>\n<ul><li>Price basis (for example FOB or CIF, depending on your arrangement).</li><li>Payment terms and schedule.</li><li>Currency.</li><li>Who pays for packing, documentation and inspection.</li></ul>\n<p>Compare quotes carefully on a like-for-like basis. A lower price may reflect lower knot density or cheaper materials.</p>\n<h2>Step 8: Plan quality control</h2>\n<p>Ask what in-house inspection the manufacturer does at each stage. For large orders you may also appoint a third-party inspector before shipment. Check size, colour, pile height, design accuracy and finishing.</p>\n<h2>Step 9: Arrange packing and shipping</h2>\n<p>Rugs are typically rolled, wrapped in protective plastic and bundled. Sea freight suits larger volumes; air freight suits urgent or small consignments. A good manufacturer will help you with commercial invoices, packing lists and other export documents.</p>\n<h2>Step 10: Build the relationship</h2>\n<p>The best sourcing relationships are long-term. Start with a trial order, give clear feedback, pay on time and develop exclusive designs together. Over time you gain better pricing, priority in production and products no competitor has.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>\n<ol><li>Choosing purely on price.</li><li>Approving bulk production without a sample.</li><li>Vague specifications.</li><li>Ignoring lead times around holidays and peak seasons.</li><li>Not agreeing inspection standards in advance.</li></ol>\n<h2>What Tajmahal Products offers</h2>\n<p>We offer hand-knotted, hand-tufted and other handmade rugs in wool and silk across six signature collections, plus custom and private-label development. Our production combines traditional craftsmanship with modern quality-control systems, and we focus on quality products, competitive pricing, dependable delivery and personalised service.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Do I need to visit India before ordering?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily, but a visit is valuable for larger programmes. Many buyers start with samples and video calls.</p>\n<h3>Can I order small quantities to test the market?</h3>\n<p>Often yes. Discuss trial quantities with the manufacturer.</p>\n<h3>Can you make rugs under my own brand?</h3>\n<p>Yes, private-label programmes are possible.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
