    "category": "Collections",
    "readTime": "8 min",
    "wordCount": 643,
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-25",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "What is the difference between Kazak and Gabbeh rugs?",
        "a": "Kazaks are geometric, with dense pile and strong medallions. Gabbehs are simpler, coarser and softer, with open fields."
      },
      {
        "q": "Are Kazak rugs always red?",
        "a": "No. Red is traditional, but modern colourways range from soft neutrals to deep blues and greens."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can Kazak rugs be made in custom sizes?",
        "a": "Yes, custom sizes and colours are common in made-to-order production."
      }
    ],
    "content": "<p>Kazak rugs are among the boldest of all oriental carpets. Large geometric medallions, strong colour contrasts and a thick, dense pile give them a presence that few other styles match. This guide explains the history and design of Kazak rugs, how to recognise quality and how to use them in modern interiors. It comes from Tajmahal Products, whose Kazzak collection brings tribal geometry and dense pile to buyers worldwide.</p>\n<h2>Where do Kazak rugs come from?</h2>\n<p>The name Kazak is linked to a region in the southern Caucasus and the tribes who wove there. Caucasian weaving is known for strong, angular designs, a result of the way geometric forms are tied on a loom. Kazak rugs were traditionally woven by village weavers in wool, using natural dyes, and are prized for their vigour and directness.</p>\n<p>Over time, the Kazak style has been adopted by weavers in other countries, including India, who produce Kazak-inspired carpets in a wide range of sizes and colours.</p>\n<h2>Design features</h2>\n<h3>Bold medallions</h3>\n<p>The classic Kazak layout has a few large medallions, often diamonds, stars or hooked forms, arranged down the centre of the field.</p>\n<h3>Geometric borders</h3>\n<p>Borders typically repeat simple shapes such as zigzags, stepped forms and stylised plants.</p>\n<h3>Strong colour</h3>\n<p>Red, blue, ivory, green and gold are typical. Colour is used in blocks, giving the rugs a graphic quality.</p>\n<h3>Thick pile</h3>\n<p>Traditional Kazaks had a high, lustrous wool pile that is soft and substantial.</p>\n<h2>Why Kazak rugs suit modern homes</h2>\n<p>The very boldness of Kazak rugs is what makes them versatile. In a minimalist room, a Kazak provides a vivid, graphic anchor. In a traditional space, it adds rustic character. Mixed with contemporary furniture, the geometry looks fresh rather than dated.</p>\n<h2>How Kazak-style rugs are made</h2>\n<p>Kazak-style carpets are generally hand-knotted using the symmetrical knot, which gives clean geometric lines and a sturdy structure. The design is woven from a graph plan, and yarn is dyed to match specified colours. Quality depends on precise knotting of straight lines and good dyeing to keep the colour blocks crisp.</p>\n<h2>Judging quality</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Clarity of design:</strong> straight lines and clean angles, with no blurring between colours.</li><li><strong>Pile:</strong> dense, even and lustrous.</li><li><strong>Colour:</strong> saturated yet balanced; no streaking or bleeding.</li><li><strong>Edges and ends:</strong> well finished and straight.</li><li><strong>Back of the rug:</strong> knots should be visible and regular.</li></ul>\n<h2>Where to use a Kazak rug</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Living rooms:</strong> place under a coffee table and seating to set the tone.</li><li><strong>Dining rooms:</strong> the dense pile and busy pattern hide crumbs and marks well.</li><li><strong>Studies and libraries:</strong> the warm colours complement wood and books.</li><li><strong>Entrances:</strong> a durable Kazak makes a strong first impression.</li></ol>\n<h2>Styling advice</h2>\n<p>Pair a Kazak with solid-coloured upholstery to let the rug shine. Pick out one accent colour from the rug for cushions or throws. In a neutral room, a single Kazak provides all the colour you need.</p>\n<h2>Caring for a Kazak rug</h2>\n<p>Vacuum gently, avoid harsh brushes, blot spills quickly and keep out of strong direct sunlight to protect colours. Use a rug pad to prevent slipping and rotate occasionally for even wear.</p>\n<h2>Ordering Kazak-style rugs</h2>\n<p>Because the designs are geometric and clear, they adapt well to custom colourways and sizes. At Tajmahal Products we develop Kazzak rugs to order, with options for size, colour and construction, for retailers, importers and designers.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>What is the difference between Kazak and Gabbeh rugs?</h3>\n<p>Kazaks are geometric, with dense pile and strong medallions. Gabbehs are simpler, coarser and softer, with open fields.</p>\n<h3>Are Kazak rugs always red?</h3>\n<p>No. Red is traditional, but modern colourways range from soft neutrals to deep blues and greens.</p>\n<h3>Can Kazak rugs be made in custom sizes?</h3>\n<p>Yes, custom sizes and colours are common in made-to-order production.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
