    "id": 8,
    "slug": "wool-carpets-complete-guide-fibre-quality-and-care",
    "title": "Wool Carpets: The Complete Guide to Fibre Quality, Properties, Benefits and Care",
    "metaDescription": "Why wool is the world's favourite carpet fibre: properties, grades, spinning, dyeing, benefits, drawbacks, wool vs synthetic comparison and care tips from a Bhadohi carpet manufacturer.",
    "keywords": [
      "wool carpets",
      "wool rug benefits",
      "wool vs synthetic rugs",
      "best wool for carpets",
      "wool carpet quality"
    ],
    "category": "Materials",
    "readTime": "9 min",
    "wordCount": 709,
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-28",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Is wool better than synthetic for rugs?",
        "a": "For longevity, feel and natural qualities, wool generally outperforms synthetics, though synthetics can be useful in specific budget or outdoor cases."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why does my new wool rug shed?",
        "a": "Loose fibres left from manufacturing come off during early use. Vacuum regularly and it will settle."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can wool rugs be used in kitchens?",
        "a": "They can, but spills and moisture need attention. Use caution and quick cleaning."
      }
    ],
    "content": "<p>Walk into any serious carpet showroom and most of the best pieces will be made of wool. There are good reasons: wool has an unusual combination of strength, resilience, warmth and beauty that synthetics struggle to match. This guide explains why wool is the preferred carpet fibre, how quality varies, and how to look after a wool carpet. It is written by Tajmahal Products, a Bhadohi manufacturer specialising in handmade wool and silk rugs.</p>\n<h2>Why wool?</h2>\n<h3>Natural resilience</h3>\n<p>Wool fibres are crimped, like tiny springs. When walked on, they compress and bounce back, which helps a wool carpet keep its texture over years of use.</p>\n<h3>Durability</h3>\n<p>Wool is a strong fibre and can be flexed many times without breaking, making it ideal for floors.</p>\n<h3>Dye affinity</h3>\n<p>Wool absorbs dye deeply and holds colour well, allowing a rich range of shades.</p>\n<h3>Warmth and comfort</h3>\n<p>Wool insulates against cold and softens sound, making rooms feel warmer and quieter.</p>\n<h3>Natural stain resistance</h3>\n<p>Wool has a protective outer layer and a natural waxy coating that helps liquids bead up briefly, giving time to blot spills.</p>\n<h3>Renewable</h3>\n<p>Wool is a natural, renewable material and is biodegradable at the end of its life.</p>\n<h2>What affects wool quality</h2>\n<h3>Breed and source</h3>\n<p>Different sheep breeds produce fibres of different fineness, length and lustre. Long, strong fibres suit hard-wearing carpets.</p>\n<h3>Staple length</h3>\n<p>Longer fibres (staple) give stronger yarn with less shedding.</p>\n<h3>Fineness</h3>\n<p>Finer wool gives a softer handle and allows finer knotting.</p>\n<h3>Cleanliness and processing</h3>\n<p>Wool must be thoroughly scoured (washed) to remove grease and dirt. Poorly cleaned wool can smell, attract insects and dye unevenly.</p>\n<h2>From fleece to yarn</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Shearing and sorting:</strong> fleece is sorted by quality.</li><li><strong>Scouring:</strong> washing to remove lanolin, dirt and plant matter.</li><li><strong>Carding:</strong> fibres are combed into aligned sheets.</li><li><strong>Spinning:</strong> fibres are twisted into yarn. Handspun yarn is more irregular and rustic; machine-spun yarn is smoother and more uniform.</li><li><strong>Dyeing:</strong> yarn is dyed in batches and checked against approved shades.</li></ol>\n<h2>Wool vs other fibres</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Fibre</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wool</td><td>Resilient, durable, warm, rich colour</td><td>Higher cost, needs moth care</td></tr><tr><td>Silk</td><td>Lustrous, very fine detail</td><td>Delicate, costly</td></tr><tr><td>Cotton</td><td>Strong, stable (foundations), affordable</td><td>Lacks resilience in pile</td></tr><tr><td>Viscose</td><td>Silky sheen, lower cost</td><td>Weak when wet, wears faster</td></tr><tr><td>Polyester / polypropylene</td><td>Cheap, stain-resistant</td><td>Less natural feel, can mat</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Wool is typically the best choice where long-term performance and a natural feel matter.</p>\n<h2>Wool carpets and allergies</h2>\n<p>Some people worry that wool is bad for allergies. In fact, the dense structure of a clean wool rug can trap dust, so regular vacuuming matters. Sensitivities vary; people with wool allergies should test before buying.</p>\n<h2>Shedding and pilling</h2>\n<p>New wool rugs shed loose fibres for the first weeks or months. This is normal, not a sign of poor quality, and it reduces with regular vacuuming. Pilling (small balls of fibre) is more common in loosely spun yarn and softer wool; good yarn spinning reduces it.</p>\n<h2>Caring for wool carpets</h2>\n<ul><li>Vacuum regularly using suction, avoiding harsh beater bars on delicate pile.</li><li>Blot spills, never rub.</li><li>Use a quality rug pad.</li><li>Rotate rugs to spread wear and sun exposure.</li><li>Protect from moths by keeping the rug clean and unfolded in storage.</li><li>Arrange professional cleaning every few years, depending on traffic.</li></ul>\n<h2>Wool in Tajmahal Products rugs</h2>\n<p>Our hand-knotted, hand-tufted and handwoven rugs use wool selected for strength and softness, dyed to approved standards, then washed and finished with care. We also work with silk and blends where a project calls for added sheen.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Is wool better than synthetic for rugs?</h3>\n<p>For longevity, feel and natural qualities, wool generally outperforms synthetics, though synthetics can be useful in specific budget or outdoor cases.</p>\n<h3>Why does my new wool rug shed?</h3>\n<p>Loose fibres left from manufacturing come off during early use. Vacuum regularly and it will settle.</p>\n<h3>Can wool rugs be used in kitchens?</h3>\n<p>They can, but spills and moisture need attention. Use caution and quick cleaning.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
