    "id": 9,
    "slug": "silk-carpets-guide-luxury-hand-knotted-silk-rugs",
    "title": "Silk Carpets: A Guide to Luxury, Fine Knotting, Care and Value",
    "metaDescription": "Everything about silk carpets: why silk is prized, types of silk, fine knot density, wool-and-silk blends, how to care for silk rugs and how to judge quality and value.",
    "keywords": [
      "silk carpets",
      "silk rugs",
      "hand knotted silk rugs",
      "silk carpet care",
      "wool and silk rugs"
    ],
    "category": "Materials",
    "readTime": "8 min",
    "wordCount": 767,
    "publishedAt": "2026-10-29",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Are silk carpets durable?",
        "a": "Silk fibre is strong but sensitive to abrasion and moisture. In low-traffic areas, a good silk carpet can last for generations."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is the difference between silk and art silk?",
        "a": "Silk is a natural protein fibre from silkworms; art silk is usually viscose made from cellulose, with a similar look but less durability."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can I order a wool and silk blend?",
        "a": "Yes. Blends are popular and can be tailored to design and budget."
      }
    ],
    "content": "<p>Silk is the most luxurious carpet material. It catches light in a way no other fibre does, shifting from pale to deep shade as you move around the room. Silk also allows extraordinary precision in design, which is why the finest carpets in the world are often silk. This guide explains what makes silk special, how silk carpets are made, how to judge quality and how to care for them. It is published by Tajmahal Products, whose portfolio is crafted in wool and silk.</p>\n<h2>Why silk is special</h2>\n<h3>Lustre</h3>\n<p>Silk fibres are smooth and reflective, creating a distinctive sheen. Because the fibres lie in different directions in the pile, the carpet appears to change colour depending on the angle of the light.</p>\n<h3>Fineness</h3>\n<p>Silk yarn is very thin and strong, so it can be knotted at extremely high densities. This allows tiny detail: delicate petals, fine calligraphy and intricate medallions.</p>\n<h3>Smooth handle</h3>\n<p>Silk feels cool, smooth and fine to the touch.</p>\n<h3>Rich colour</h3>\n<p>Silk takes dye brilliantly, producing jewel-like colours.</p>\n<h2>Types of silk carpets</h2>\n<h3>Pure silk carpets</h3>\n<p>Pile and foundation both silk. These are the most delicate and costly, best displayed on a wall or placed in a quiet room.</p>\n<h3>Silk pile on cotton foundation</h3>\n<p>Silk used for the pile with cotton warps and wefts for stability. This is a common construction.</p>\n<h3>Wool and silk blends</h3>\n<p>Wool provides body and durability, while silk is used for highlights in flowers, outlines or borders. The contrast between matte wool and shining silk gives depth and drama.</p>\n<h3>Art silk (viscose)</h3>\n<p>Not true silk. Viscose has a silky look at a lower price but is less durable and weaker when wet. Good sellers clearly state when art silk is used.</p>\n<h2>How silk carpets are made</h2>\n<p>The process is similar to other hand-knotted carpets but requires exceptional care. Yarn is carefully spun and dyed, the loom is strung with fine warps, and weavers tie extremely small knots at high density. Because silk is delicate, clipping and washing need skilled hands to avoid damage. The finest silk carpets can take a very long time to complete.</p>\n<h2>Judging silk carpet quality</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Lustre:</strong> real silk shimmers softly and shifts colour with angle. Overly shiny, plastic-like sheen can suggest viscose.</li><li><strong>Knot density:</strong> high density gives sharp detail; look at lines and curves for clarity.</li><li><strong>Colour clarity:</strong> shades should be clean and even.</li><li><strong>Back of the carpet:</strong> a clear, regular pattern shows careful work.</li><li><strong>Edges and finish:</strong> neat, straight and secure.</li></ul>\n<p>A simple home check often used by experts is the burn test on a loose thread: real silk smells like burnt hair and leaves crushable ash, while viscose smells like burning paper. Do not do this on a carpet you intend to keep, and ask the seller for fibre information instead.</p>\n<h2>Where to use silk carpets</h2>\n<p>Silk rugs suit low-traffic spaces: formal living rooms, bedrooms, studies, boutique hotel suites and display areas. They are also used as wall hangings and as feature pieces in collections. In high-traffic areas, wool or wool and silk blends are a more practical choice.</p>\n<h2>Care for silk carpets</h2>\n<ol><li>Vacuum gently, preferably with a low suction setting and no beater bar.</li><li>Blot spills immediately with a clean absorbent cloth. Do not rub.</li><li>Avoid direct sunlight, which can fade silk.</li><li>Avoid heavy furniture on silk areas, or use protective cups.</li><li>Use professional cleaners experienced with silk.</li><li>Do not use harsh chemicals or hot water extraction.</li></ol>\n<h2>Is a silk carpet a good investment?</h2>\n<p>The value of a silk carpet depends on quality of materials, density, design, condition and market demand. Buy because you love it and will use it appropriately, and be wary of sellers who promise guaranteed financial returns.</p>\n<h2>Silk at Tajmahal Products</h2>\n<p>We produce handmade rugs in wool and silk, including refined constructions where higher knot density and richer finish are required. Our Mughal Super collection is one example of how finer knotting and finishing can raise a design to a higher level.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Are silk carpets durable?</h3>\n<p>Silk fibre is strong but sensitive to abrasion and moisture. In low-traffic areas, a good silk carpet can last for generations.</p>\n<h3>What is the difference between silk and art silk?</h3>\n<p>Silk is a natural protein fibre from silkworms; art silk is usually viscose made from cellulose, with a similar look but less durability.</p>\n<h3>Can I order a wool and silk blend?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Blends are popular and can be tailored to design and budget.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
