    "id": 12,
    "slug": "rug-size-guide-room-by-room",
    "title": "Rug Size Guide: How to Choose the Right Rug Size for Every Room in Your Home",
    "metaDescription": "A detailed rug size guide for living rooms, bedrooms, dining rooms, hallways, kitchens and offices, with standard sizes, placement rules and common mistakes to avoid.",
    "keywords": [
      "rug size guide",
      "what size rug do I need",
      "living room rug size",
      "bedroom rug size",
      "dining room rug size"
    ],
    "category": "Buying Guides",
    "readTime": "10 min",
    "wordCount": 950,
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-01",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Should a rug go under all furniture legs?",
        "a": "Not necessarily. At least the front legs should be on the rug to tie the group together."
      },
      {
        "q": "How much floor should show around a rug?",
        "a": "Roughly 15 to 45 cm (6 to 18 inches), depending on room size."
      },
      {
        "q": "What size is best for a living room?",
        "a": "For most living rooms, 8 x 10 or 9 x 12 works well."
      }
    ],
    "content": "<p>Choosing the wrong rug size is the single most common rug mistake. Too small, and a rug looks like a postage stamp floating in the room; too large, and it fights with walls and doors. This guide gives you practical rules and standard sizes for every room, plus advice on custom sizing. It is published by Tajmahal Products, a manufacturer that makes rugs in custom sizes for homes, retailers and hospitality projects.</p>\n<h2>Golden rules of rug sizing</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Go bigger than you think.</strong> A larger rug makes a room feel more generous.</li><li><strong>Anchor furniture.</strong> At least the front legs of main furniture should sit on the rug.</li><li><strong>Leave a border.</strong> Usually 15 to 45 cm (6 to 18 inches) of floor around the rug looks neat.</li><li><strong>Consider doors and traffic.</strong> Make sure doors can open freely and that the rug does not catch.</li><li><strong>Measure and mark.</strong> Use painter's tape on the floor to preview the rug's size before you buy.</li></ol>\n<h2>Common standard rug sizes</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Imperial (feet)</th><th>Approx. metric (cm)</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 x 3</td><td>60 x 90</td><td>Doormat, bath mat</td></tr><tr><td>3 x 5</td><td>90 x 150</td><td>Entry, small accent</td></tr><tr><td>4 x 6</td><td>120 x 180</td><td>Small accent, desk area</td></tr><tr><td>5 x 8</td><td>150 x 240</td><td>Small living room, bedroom side</td></tr><tr><td>6 x 9</td><td>180 x 270</td><td>Small dining, mid living room</td></tr><tr><td>8 x 10</td><td>240 x 300</td><td>Standard living room, bedroom</td></tr><tr><td>9 x 12</td><td>270 x 360</td><td>Large living room, dining room</td></tr><tr><td>10 x 14</td><td>300 x 420</td><td>Large rooms, open plan</td></tr><tr><td>Runners 2.5 x 8, 2.5 x 10</td><td>75 x 240, 75 x 300</td><td>Hallways, kitchens</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Sizes vary by region and manufacturer, and handmade rugs may differ slightly from the nominal size.</p>\n<h2>Living room</h2>\n<p>Three layouts work well:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>All legs on:</strong> all furniture legs rest on the rug. This feels luxurious and suits large rooms (typically 9 x 12 or larger).</li><li><strong>Front legs on:</strong> the front legs of sofas and chairs sit on the rug, the back legs off. The most common approach, usually 8 x 10.</li><li><strong>Floating:</strong> only the coffee table sits on the rug. This is only advisable in compact spaces and generally makes a room feel smaller.</li></ul>\n<p>Make sure the rug extends beyond the sides of the sofa by at least 15 cm.</p>\n<h2>Bedroom</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>King bed:</strong> a 9 x 12 or 8 x 10 rug placed under the lower two-thirds of the bed so it extends at least 60 cm (2 feet) at the sides and foot.</li><li><strong>Queen bed:</strong> an 8 x 10 rug, same placement.</li><li><strong>Double or single bed:</strong> a 5 x 8 or 6 x 9 rug works; or use two runners on either side.</li></ul>\n<p>Another option is to place a rug at the foot of the bed only, which works for smaller rooms.</p>\n<h2>Dining room</h2>\n<p>The golden rule is that chairs should remain on the rug even when pulled out. Measure the table and add at least 60 cm (24 inches) on all sides. For a 6-seat rectangular table, a 8 x 10 rug is typically right; for larger tables, 9 x 12. A round table suits a round or square rug.</p>\n<h2>Hallways and entrances</h2>\n<p>Use a runner that leaves 10 to 15 cm of floor visible on each side and ends 10 to 20 cm short of each end. Choose a low-pile or flatweave style that is easy to vacuum and does not catch doors.</p>\n<h2>Kitchen</h2>\n<p>Choose a runner or small rug in front of the sink or cooking area. Look for a thin, washable, low-profile rug with a non-slip pad.</p>\n<h2>Home office</h2>\n<p>The rug should be big enough that the desk chair stays on the rug when you push back. Typically 5 x 8 or 6 x 9. Avoid a thick pile under a rolling chair.</p>\n<h2>Children's rooms</h2>\n<p>Choose a large rug for play space, such as 6 x 9 or 8 x 10, with a medium pile and a washable or durable fibre.</p>\n<h2>Round, square and irregular rugs</h2>\n<p>Round rugs suit round tables, entry halls and reading nooks. Square rugs work in square rooms or under square tables. Organic shapes add a modern touch to living rooms and bedrooms.</p>\n<h2>Open-plan spaces</h2>\n<p>Use rugs to define zones: one for the living area, another for dining. Keep the rugs related in colour or texture so the space feels cohesive.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ol><li>Choosing a rug that is too small.</li><li>Ignoring door clearance.</li><li>Not accounting for pile height under furniture.</li><li>Forgetting the rug pad (which adds to the thickness).</li><li>Overlooking proportions: the rug should relate to the room, not just the furniture.</li></ol>\n<h2>Custom sizes</h2>\n<p>If standard sizes do not fit your room, a custom rug solves the problem. One of the biggest advantages of buying from a manufacturer is that sizes, shapes and proportions can be tailored. Tajmahal Products makes rugs in custom sizes and shapes for homes, retail ranges and hospitality projects.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Should a rug go under all furniture legs?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. At least the front legs should be on the rug to tie the group together.</p>\n<h3>How much floor should show around a rug?</h3>\n<p>Roughly 15 to 45 cm (6 to 18 inches), depending on room size.</p>\n<h3>What size is best for a living room?</h3>\n<p>For most living rooms, 8 x 10 or 9 x 12 works well.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
