    "title": "Private Label Rugs: The Complete Guide for Retailers, Importers and E-Commerce Brands",
    "metaDescription": "How to build a private label rug brand: choosing a manufacturer, creating exclusive designs, specifications, sampling, labelling, packaging, pricing, MOQ and quality control.",
    "keywords": [
      "private label rugs",
      "white label rugs",
      "private label carpet manufacturer",
      "own brand rugs India",
      "rug brand startup"
    ],
    "category": "Custom & Private Label",
    "readTime": "10 min",
    "wordCount": 771,
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-09",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Do I need to supply my own designs?",
        "a": "No. You can choose from existing designs, adapt them or commission new ones."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can I use my own labels and packaging?",
        "a": "Yes, branding and packaging can be arranged as part of a private label programme."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is there a minimum order?",
        "a": "Minimums depend on construction and customisation. Discuss your plan so a workable starting quantity can be agreed."
      }
    ],
    "content": "<p>Selling the same rugs as every other retailer is a race to the bottom on price. Private label gives retailers, importers and online brands a way out: exclusive designs, your own branding and control over quality and margin. This guide explains how private label rug programmes work and how to set one up with a manufacturer. It is published by Tajmahal Products, which develops custom and private-label work for retailers, importers, wholesalers and designers.</p>\n<h2>What is private label?</h2>\n<p>In a private label programme, a manufacturer makes rugs to your specifications and sells them to you for resale under your own brand. You may supply designs, choose from the manufacturer's library, or collaborate on new designs. The rugs carry your label, hangtag and packaging.</p>\n<h2>Why choose private label</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Exclusivity:</strong> designs that competitors cannot stock.</li><li><strong>Margin:</strong> direct manufacturing lowers cost compared with buying through layers.</li><li><strong>Brand building:</strong> consistent style and quality across a collection.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> you decide sizes, colours, materials and finishing.</li><li><strong>Pricing power:</strong> unique products avoid direct price comparison.</li></ol>\n<h2>Who uses private label</h2>\n<ul><li>Independent rug retailers building signature collections.</li><li>Furniture and home stores adding rug ranges.</li><li>Online brands and marketplace sellers.</li><li>Interior design firms with their own product lines.</li><li>Hospitality groups wanting consistent rugs across properties.</li></ul>\n<h2>Step-by-step: launching a private label programme</h2>\n<h3>Step 1: Define your brand and customer</h3>\n<p>Who are you selling to? What price range, style and quality level? A clear brand direction prevents a scattered collection.</p>\n<h3>Step 2: Choose constructions</h3>\n<p>Match construction to positioning:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hand-knotted:</strong> premium, heirloom positioning.</li><li><strong>Hand-tufted:</strong> accessible price, fast turnaround, large sizes.</li><li><strong>Flatweave and handloom:</strong> casual and lifestyle ranges.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: Develop designs</h3>\n<p>Options include selecting from the manufacturer's existing designs, adapting a design with new colours, commissioning original designs or supplying your own artwork. Build a coherent collection with a mix of hero designs and supporting neutrals.</p>\n<h3>Step 4: Specify materials and quality</h3>\n<p>Decide on wool, silk or blends, pile height, density, colourfastness expectations and finishing details, and put them in writing.</p>\n<h3>Step 5: Sample and approve</h3>\n<p>Request samples or strike-offs of each design and colour. Check size, colour under your lighting, pile height and finish. Sign off samples formally; they become the reference for production.</p>\n<h3>Step 6: Agree commercial terms</h3>\n<p>Discuss MOQs per design, price per size, payment terms, lead time, delivery terms and who covers labels and packaging.</p>\n<h3>Step 7: Brand the product</h3>\n<p>Typical branding options include woven or printed labels sewn on the back, hangtags with your story and care instructions, custom packaging and swing tickets, and barcodes and size labels for retail systems.</p>\n<h3>Step 8: Production and quality control</h3>\n<p>Confirm inspection points and standards. For large orders, consider third-party inspection before dispatch.</p>\n<h3>Step 9: Shipping and delivery</h3>\n<p>Arrange packing, documentation and freight. Allow buffer for seasonal peaks.</p>\n<h3>Step 10: Launch and refine</h3>\n<p>Track sales, return reasons and customer feedback, then refine the range. Add new designs regularly to keep the collection fresh.</p>\n<h2>Pricing your private label rugs</h2>\n<p>Price from cost upward, including manufacturing, freight, duties, packaging, marketing and your target margin. Compare your price with similar quality in your market, not just the cheapest options. Unique designs allow stronger pricing.</p>\n<h2>Protecting your designs</h2>\n<p>If you provide original designs, discuss confidentiality and exclusivity with your manufacturer in writing. Agree whether a design is exclusive to you and for how long.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ol><li>Launching too many designs at once.</li><li>Skipping sample approval.</li><li>Vague specifications.</li><li>Ignoring seasonality and lead times.</li><li>Not budgeting for packaging and labelling.</li><li>Choosing a manufacturer purely on price.</li></ol>\n<h2>What to look for in a private label partner</h2>\n<ul><li>Experience with export and private-label work.</li><li>Flexible design and colour development.</li><li>Clear quality-control systems.</li><li>Transparent communication.</li><li>Dependable delivery.</li><li>Willingness to grow with you.</li></ul>\n<h2>Private label with Tajmahal Products</h2>\n<p>We are a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter based in Bhadohi, in the international export market since 1967. We develop custom designs, sizes, colours and constructions for retailers, importers, wholesalers, designers and hospitality projects, combining traditional handcraftsmanship with modern quality-control systems.</p>\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n<h3>Do I need to supply my own designs?</h3>\n<p>No. You can choose from existing designs, adapt them or commission new ones.</p>\n<h3>Can I use my own labels and packaging?</h3>\n<p>Yes, branding and packaging can be arranged as part of a private label programme.</p>\n<h3>Is there a minimum order?</h3>\n<p>Minimums depend on construction and customisation. Discuss your plan so a workable starting quantity can be agreed.</p>\n<div class=\"blog-cta\"><p><strong>Looking for handmade rugs from Bhadohi, India?</strong> Tajmahal Products is a sixth-generation, family-owned manufacturer and exporter of handmade carpets and rugs, established 1967. For custom and private-label enquiries, email <a href=\"mailto:tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com\">tmproducts.bhadohi@gmail.com</a> or call +91 82995 01435.</p></div>",
